> API and interpretation

This is a fixed-prime radix-two convolution with explicit root and coefficient
limits. It is not an arbitrary-precision implementation of the original
Schönhage–Strassen ring method. Complete operands are validated before
leading-zero normalization or zero shortcuts. The actual root powers delegate to
the canonical modular-power producer once per requested root.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Cooley and Tukey, An Algorithm for the Machine Calculation of Complex Fourier Series (1965), radix-two transform background|https://web.stanford.edu/class/cme324/classics/cooley-tukey.pdf]

